        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Foam Dome, Hard Shell or Soft Sock: Which Faucet Cover Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every outdoor faucet cover does the same basic job: it traps a pocket of still air around the spigot and holds in the small amount of heat that leaks out through the wall from the house. The differences are in how well each type seals, how long it lasts and what shapes of faucet it fits. Three designs cover almost everything sold for hose bibs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foam Domes</h3>

          <p>
            The classic faucet cover is a rigid foam dome, like the Frost King FC1 or the Biggun. A loop inside hooks over the faucet, and a cord, toggle or drawstring pulls the dome tight so its foam gasket presses against the siding. Foam domes are cheap, light and effective on a standard hose bib, because the gasket stops wind getting underneath. Their weakness is the foam: it dents and cracks if it is knocked, and it will not close over a faucet that is too big for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hard Shell Covers</h3>

          <p>
            Hard shell covers, like the Frost King FC2 and the Zoblise, put a plastic outer skin over a foam liner. The foam insulates and the shell takes the knocks from shovels, mowers and pets, so they last longer by a walkway or driveway. They cost a little more and are bulkier, but on an exposed spigot they are the most durable choice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soft Insulated Socks</h3>

          <p>
            Soft covers, like the Hedoc Pro, ArtiGifts Pro and Duck Soft Flexible, are padded fabric pouches that drop over the faucet and close with hook-and-loop straps or a tie. Because the fabric flexes, they fit long handles, vacuum breakers and odd shapes that a rigid dome cannot. The trade-off is the seal: a fabric edge does not press against the wall as firmly as a foam gasket, and a soaked sock insulates far less than a dry one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="do-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Do Faucet Covers Work? Yes, for the Spigot, Not the Pipe in the Wall
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the question most people should ask before buying, and the honest answer has a limit built in. A faucet cover protects the part of the faucet you can see. It does not protect the pipe running through the wall, the crawl space or the garage behind it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Cover Actually Does</h3>

          <p>
            An outdoor spigot is a short run of metal sticking out into the cold, connected to a pipe that carries heat from the house. Bare, the spigot loses that heat to wind and cold air in minutes. Under a sealed cover, the trapped air warms slightly from the wall and the faucet stays above freezing for longer. On a typical freezing night, that difference is often enough to stop the faucet body from freezing and cracking.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Cover Cannot Do</h3>

          <p>
            A cover cannot add heat. If the pipe behind the wall runs through an unheated crawl space, garage or rim joist, it can freeze and split there no matter what is on the outside. A cover also cannot help a faucet that still has a hose attached, because the hose traps water in the valve. And in a long, deep cold snap, a covered faucet will eventually reach the outside temperature.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Cover Is Enough</h3>

          <p>
            A cover is the right extra layer on a frost-free sillcock, or on a standard faucet whose indoor shut-off valve has been closed and the line drained. It is a stopgap on a standard faucet with no indoor shut-off. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-winterize-outdoor-faucets/">how to winterize outdoor faucets</a> walks through shutting off, draining and insulating in the right order.
          </p>

